                <note rend="inline" type="source">438.00/263</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the High Commissioner in
                        Haiti</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Russell</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1923-07-09"><hi rendition="#italic">July 9,
                        1923</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 116</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to your cable No. 77, June 12,
                    noon, in which you inform the Department of the French Minister’s suggestion
                    that three French claims arising since May, 1916, be referred to the arbitral
                    tribunal to be appointed to consider French claims prior to 1913, and of
                    President Borno’s statement that the same treatment will be accorded to the
                    claims of the nationals of other governments if they so desire, the Department
                    advises you that it perceives no objection to the procedure suggested.</p>
                <p>I am [etc.]</p>
                <closer>For the Secretary of State:<lb/><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">William
                            Phillips</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
